<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Shahanshah</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Shahanshah, meaning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/negus-nagast">King of Kings</a>, was the title of the supreme ruler in ancient and medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. It was used by the Achaemenid kings and revived by the Sasanian dynasty, whose founder <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardashir-i">Ardashir I</a> adopted it. Accounts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sasanian-empire">Sasanian Empire</a> treat the title as the expression of the monarch's supreme authority over lesser kings and nobles.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/shahanshah.jpg" alt="Shahanshah" width="289" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sotheby's · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_portrait_of_Agha_Muhammad_Khan,_Persia,_Qajar,_dated_1795.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
